The full paper confirms that its central finite results are constructive, not complexity results. In particular, Theorem 2.1 produces a minimum set by choosing one edge in each factor and taking any \(r\) vertices of the resulting \(2^k\)-vertex product clique; closure verification is linear in the product graph. That rules out a Track A claim for the naïve planted-set family. I’m now testing the only credible Track B formulation: hide the strong-product coordinates under an exact relabelling and require a compact factorization certificate that deterministically yields the minimum percolating set.
